\subsection{Erase Pattern}

Reimplementation of ERASE\_PATTERN at \$272D. Reads the old magic control byte,
old screen address, and old pattern pointer from the VECTOR struct, then
calls [[draw\_sprite]] to XOR-erase the previous sprite image.

The Z80 stores these ``old'' values during the previous WRITE\_PATTERN call
so that the erase uses exactly the same parameters. After erasing, the
ERASE status bit is cleared so it is not done twice.

<<native erase pattern>>=
void BerzerkNative::erase_pattern(Vector& v)
{
    if (!(v.status & STATUS_ERASE)) return;
    v.status &= ~STATUS_ERASE;

    // Restore the magic control byte that was active when the sprite was drawn
    m_video.magic.control = v.magic;
    m_video.magic.last_shift_data = 0;
    m_video.magic.intercept = 1;

    // old_pattern_addr points into ROM pattern data (absolute ROM address)
    if (v.old_pattern_addr == 0) return;
    const uint8_t* pattern = &m_program_rom[v.old_pattern_addr];

    draw_sprite(pattern, v.old_screen_addr);
}
@

\subsection{Write Pattern}

Reimplementation of WRITE\_PATTERN at \$274D. Computes a Magic RAM address
from the VECTOR's current (P.X, P.Y) position using XOR mode (\$90), then
follows the double-indirection pattern pointer to find the actual sprite
data, draws it, and checks the intercept register for collision.

The pattern pointer follows double indirection:
\begin{enumerate}
\item D.P points to an animation table entry (2 bytes: pointer to frame data).
\item The frame data pointer may have bit~7 set, indicating a position offset
      (2 bytes: signed BC) to add to the screen address before drawing.
\end{enumerate}

After drawing, the old screen address, magic control, and pattern pointer
are saved into the VECTOR so that ERASE\_PATTERN can undo the draw later.

<<native write pattern>>=
void BerzerkNative::write_pattern(Vector& v)
{
    if (!(v.status & STATUS_WRITE)) return;
    v.status &= ~STATUS_WRITE;

    // Compute magic RAM address from (P.X, P.Y) with XOR mode ($90)
    uint8_t shift = v.px & 0x07;
    uint8_t magic_ctrl = shift | 0x90;
    m_video.magic.control = magic_ctrl;
    m_video.magic.last_shift_data = 0;
    m_video.magic.intercept = 1;

    uint16_t screen_addr = calc_magic_ram_addr(v.py, v.px);

    // Save magic control byte into VECTOR for later erase
    v.magic = magic_ctrl;

    // Follow double indirection: D.P -> [animation table entry] -> sprite data
    // D.P is a ROM address pointing to a 2-byte pointer
    uint16_t anim_entry_addr = v.pattern_ptr;
    uint16_t frame_addr = static_cast<uint16_t>(m_program_rom[anim_entry_addr]) |
                          (static_cast<uint16_t>(m_program_rom[anim_entry_addr + 1]) @<< 8);

    // Check if first byte of frame data has bit 7 set (position offset present)
    uint8_t first_byte = m_program_rom[frame_addr];
    const uint8_t* pattern;
    if (first_byte & 0x80) {
        // Position offset: next 2 bytes are signed offset (low, high)
        int16_t offset = static_cast<int16_t>(
            static_cast<uint16_t>(first_byte & 0x7F) |
            (static_cast<uint16_t>(m_program_rom[frame_addr + 1]) @<< 8));
        screen_addr = static_cast<uint16_t>(screen_addr + offset);
        pattern = &m_program_rom[frame_addr + 2];
    } else {
        pattern = &m_program_rom[frame_addr];
    }

    // Save old values for erase
    v.old_pattern_addr = static_cast<uint16_t>(pattern - m_program_rom.data());
    v.old_screen_addr = screen_addr;

    // Draw the sprite
    draw_sprite(pattern, screen_addr);

    // Check collision via intercept register (port $4E)
    uint8_t intercept = read_intercept(m_video);
    if (intercept & 0x80) {
        v.status |= STATUS_HIT;
    }
}
@

\subsection{Move and Animate Vector}

Reimplementation of MOVE\_ANIMATE\_VECTOR at \$27A9. This is the core
update loop for every moving object (player, robots, Otto).

Each frame:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If STATUS\_MOVE is not set, return immediately.
\item Decrement TIME. If non-zero, return (object not ready to move yet).
\item When TIME reaches zero, reload from TPRIME.
\item Add velocity (V.X, V.Y) to position (P.X, P.Y).
\item Advance the animation frame: D.P += 2. If the new entry points to
      a zero byte, follow the 2-byte restart pointer to loop the animation.
\item OR \$1B into status (sets ERASE, WRITE, MOVE, COLOR bits).
\item If STATUS\_DYING is set, cycle the player colour.
\end{enumerate}

<<native move animate vector>>=
void BerzerkNative::move_animate_vector(Vector& v)
{
    if (!(v.status & STATUS_MOVE)) return;

    // Decrement TIME countdown
    v.time--;
    if (v.time != 0) return;

    // Reload TIME from TPRIME
    v.time = v.tprime;

    // Apply velocity to position
    v.px = static_cast<uint8_t>(v.px + v.vx);
    v.py = static_cast<uint8_t>(v.py + v.vy);

    // Advance animation frame: D.P += 2
    v.pattern_ptr += 2;

    // Check if we hit the end of the animation table (zero byte)
    uint16_t check_addr = v.pattern_ptr;
    uint8_t check = m_program_rom[check_addr];
    if (check == 0) {
        // End marker: next 2 bytes are the restart pointer
        uint16_t restart = static_cast<uint16_t>(m_program_rom[check_addr + 1]) |
                           (static_cast<uint16_t>(m_program_rom[check_addr + 2]) @<< 8);
        v.pattern_ptr = restart;
    }

    // Set status bits: ERASE | WRITE | MOVE | COLOR = $1B
    v.status |= 0x1B;

    // If DYING bit set, cycle player colour
    if (v.status & STATUS_DYING) {
        m_state.player_colour = static_cast<uint8_t>(
            (m_state.player_colour @<< 1) |
            (m_state.player_colour >> 7));
        m_state.player_colour ^= 0x11;
    }
}
